     "supermarket"/"school" example instead).

3. STRICT KNOWLEDGE SCOPE BOUNDARY (Directive 12):
{{ scope_rules }}

4. DEDICATED MULTI-INPUT PARAMETER SEPARATION DIRECTIVE:
   - If the code demo involves multiple variables/parameters:
   - ABSOLUTELY FORBIDDEN to bundle them into a single string!
   - You MUST define separate parameters in the `parameters` array with clear Vietnamese labels and IDs.

5. CONCISE, PUNCHY & MEANINGFUL TITLES:
   - `demo_title`: Keep short & punchy under 5-8 words.

6. MODULAR MICRO-STEP DEMO STRUCTURE:
   - Structure `code_snippet` into 2-3 clear, progressive, commented micro-steps.

7. MANDATORY LIVE COMPUTE LOGIC (`compute_logic`) — CRITICAL, THIS MAKES THE SIMULATOR REAL:
   - `compute_logic` MUST be a string containing raw JavaScript STATEMENTS (NOT a full function
     declaration, just the body) that will be executed at runtime via `new Function("params", compute_logic)`.
   - It receives ONE variable already in scope: `params` — a plain object whose keys are EXACTLY
     the `id` values from the `parameters` array, with live values read straight from the actual
     input/select fields the student is editing (numbers already coerced to JS `Number`).
   - It MUST actually compute the real result from `params` (mirroring the logic shown in
     `code_snippet`) — DO NOT return hardcoded/pre-baked text disconnected from `params`. The whole
     point is that when a student changes an input, `compute_logic` re-runs and the displayed
     trace/result genuinely change to match.
   - It MUST end with exactly one `return` statement of this shape:
     `return { trace: ["Bước 1: ...", "Bước 2: ...", "Bước 3: ..."], result: "<final value as string>", badge: "<short status label>" };`
     - `trace`: array of short strings, one per micro-step of `code_snippet`, computed from the
       actual `params` values (not copy-pasted from a single preset example).
     - `badge`: MUST contain a Vietnamese keyword indicating status — use "Thành công" for a normal
       successful case, "Cảnh báo"/"Biên" for an edge/boundary case, "Lỗi" for an error/invalid case
       — the UI color-codes the badge by these keywords.
   - Pure function only: no DOM access, no `eval`, no `console`, no network/timers — only read
     from `params` and return the object.
   - `scenarios[].param_values` are PRESETS that pre-fill the input fields when a student picks that
     scenario from the dropdown — they are NOT separately-rendered display text anymore; after
     pre-filling, `compute_logic` is what actually runs against those values.

